Accurate fault diagnosis of power transformers is critical for ensuring the reliability and safety of electrical power systems. While acoustic-based diagnosis offers advantages in non-contact measurement and low sensor cost, existing approaches face three key challenges: (i) reliance on single-modality representations that capture only partial fault signatures, (ii) sensitivity to background noise that degrades classification performance in real-world substation environments, and (iii) difficulty in generalizing from limited labeled data. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a multi-modal feature fusion framework that integrates Mel spectrograms with Gramian Angular Difference Fields (GADF) to construct complementary time--frequency representations. A Pulse-Coupled Neural Network (PCNN) is employed as a biologically inspired front-end to suppress noise and enhance discriminative structures through synchronized neuronal firing. Crucially, a physics-informed regularization strategy is introduced, grounded in the acoustic wave propagation physics of power transformers. Specifically, the steady-state acoustic pressure field within the transformer tank approximately satisfies the Helmholtz equation, which in homogeneous media reduces to the Laplace equation $\nabla^2 p \approx 0$ in source-free regions. This physical prior is encoded as a Laplacian smoothness constraint on the network's internal feature representations, guiding the model to distinguish physically plausible fault-induced irregularities from unstructured noise. The proposed method is evaluated on a real-world transformer acoustic dataset comprising five operational conditions, with data partitioned by independent recording sessions to prevent information leakage. Extensive experiments---including component-wise ablation studies, five-fold cross-validation, hyperparameter sensitivity analysis, and noise robustness tests---demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves state-of-the-art classification accuracy while maintaining robust performance under low signal-to-noise ratio conditions. Comparisons with seven representative methods, including Vision Transformer (ViT), EfficientNet, Swin Transformer, ConvNeXt, and end-to-end 1D-CNN, further validate the effectiveness and generalizability of the proposed approach.
